\section{Discussion and Conclusion}
\label{sec:discussion}

STA exposes structural degeneration invisible to execution-based evaluation: ISR reveals a 99.5\% collapse under exec-only GRPO that PR hides, while SFT localizes the failure to RL (\S\ref{sec:diagnostic}).
Repurposed as a training signal, ISR produces dataset-dependent outcomes determined by hacking geometry: on FOLIO, meta-level Goodhart amplifies H7 ${\sim}5{\times}$ ($p{<}10^{-39}$) and transfers to Prolog (3/3 seeds, 0\% non-hacking; \S\ref{sec:crosslogic}); on ProofFOL, beneficial instability yields lower prove rates but 76.8\% vs.\ 17.4\% genuine ($p{=}0.10$; \S\ref{sec:crossdomain}).
ISR's vulnerability is not inherent---it depends on whether dataset-specific shortcuts satisfy the metric's LOO structure.

All seeds have $\text{ISR}^*{<}0.12$---far below the pre-training baseline ($0.31$)---consistent with the LOO tautological attractor being metric-intrinsic.

\textbf{ProofFOL ISR ($\beta{=}0.1$): beneficial instability.}
ISR bonus reduces stable convergence from 2/3 to 0/3 seeds---superficially, another optimization failure.
Hacking-mode classification reverses this reading: ISR seeds average 76.8\% genuine versus 17.4\% for exec-only (permutation $p{=}0.10$, $N{=}3$), with s42 and s44 achieving 100\% genuine despite training collapse (Table~\ref{tab:prooffol-detailed}).
The models that failed to converge produced structurally honest proofs.

\textbf{Mechanism.}
Eval-time ISR confirms the asymmetry: on ProofFOL, hacking constructions yield $\text{ISR}{=}0.0$ while genuine proofs yield $\text{ISR}{=}0.27$ (s42) and $0.20$ (s44)---ISR correctly penalizes shortcuts.
On FOLIO (baseline $\text{ISR}^*{\approx}0.50$), $P{\to}P$ yields $\text{ISR}{\geq}0.5$, so ISR erroneously rewards tautological hacking (regressional Goodhart~\citep{manheim-goodhart-taxonomy}); on ProofFOL (baseline $\text{ISR}^*{=}0.31$), hacking does not satisfy ISR, so the bonus correctly suppresses shortcuts at the cost of convergence stability.
The same metric produces opposite outcomes---Goodhart collapse where hacking satisfies it (FOLIO), beneficial instability where it does not (ProofFOL)---determined by the dataset's hacking geometry.

\subsection{Hacking Taxonomy}
\label{sec:taxonomy}

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.92\columnwidth]{figures/fig3_hacking_composition.pdf}
\caption{Hacking composition across reward conditions (proved samples; exec-only: seed~42; all other bars: pooled across 3 seeds, per-seed breakdown in Appendix Table~\ref{tab:fol-detailed}). ISR converts H1 into more extreme H7 without reducing total hacking; SFT shows near-zero hacking.}
\label{fig:hacking-composition}
\end{figure}

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\resizebox{\columnwidth}{!}{%
\begin{tabular}{lccccc}
\toprule
\textbf{Mode} & \textbf{Exec} & \textbf{ISR$_{.1}$} & \textbf{Rnd$_{.1}$} & \textbf{SFT} & \textbf{Prolog$_{\text{ISR}}$} \\
\midrule
H1 embed       & 87 & 18 & 71 & 7 & 0$^\sharp$ \\
H7 duplicate   & 0  & 71 & 14 & 0 & 100$^\sharp$ \\
H8 prem-subset & 6  & 5  & 8   & 0 & --- \\
Tautological   & --- & --- & --- & --- & s42 \\
Repetition     & s43 & --- & --- & --- & --- \\
Contradiction  & 1k & --- & --- & --- & --- \\
Cmplx.\ vacuous& 0  & 0  & 4  & 0 & --- \\
Query-as-fact  & --- & --- & --- & --- & s44 \\
\midrule
\textbf{Non-hacking} & 8  & 6  & 10 & \textbf{92} & \textbf{0} \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}}
\caption{Eight hacking modes across conditions (\% of proved samples; exec-only/SFT: seed~42; ISR/Random: pooled across 3 seeds). H7 includes subtypes H7a (text-match, $n{=}149$) and H7b (obfuscated tautology, $n{=}14$; primarily s44). Cells with seed identifiers indicate the mode dominates that seed. $^\sharp$Prolog row pools strict+functional (\S\ref{sec:crosslogic}).}
\label{tab:hacking-taxonomy}
\end{table}

The three dominant modes (Table~\ref{tab:hacking-taxonomy}; definitions in Appendix~\ref{app:hacking-modes})---\textbf{H1} (conclusion embedding, GRPO-universal at $68$--$100\%$), \textbf{H7} (conclusion duplication, ISR-amplified ${\sim}5{\times}$; Fisher $p{=}1.3{\times}10^{-39}$), and \textbf{H8} (premise-subset gaming; Appendix~\ref{app:h8})---account for ${>}90\%$ of hacking across 2 logic types and 6 reward conditions.
H7 includes an obfuscated subtype \textbf{H7b} ($14/231$) that evades text-match classifiers via vacuous quantifier wrapping, simultaneously gaming execution reward, ISR, and the hacking detector (\S\ref{sec:discussion}).
The cross-condition pattern: \emph{reward formulation determines hacking composition but not prevalence}---all GRPO conditions show ${\geq}80\%$ pooled hacking; only SFT achieves $92.3\%$ non-hacking; the taxonomy is descriptive, not exhaustive (query-as-fact gaming was discovered only via functional cross-validation).

\begin{abstract}
Neurosymbolic formalizers trained with Group Relative Policy Optimization (GRPO) achieve high prove rates---93\% on FOLIO---yet 87\% of proved outputs embed the conclusion verbatim among the premises, a degenerate shortcut invisible to execution reward.
We introduce \emph{Solver Trace Ablation} (STA), a diagnostic that applies leave-one-out component necessity testing to solver inputs.
The fraction of components whose removal causes proof failure yields the \emph{Intensional Structural Reward} (ISR): ISR$=$1 indicates full necessity; ISR$=$0 complete redundancy.
ISR is uncorrelated with downstream NLI accuracy ($\rho{=}{-}0.049$), confirming it measures structural---not semantic---integrity.
As a diagnostic, ISR exposes a collapse trajectory hidden from PR: over 100--1000 GRPO steps (Qwen3-4B), ISR falls $0.642{\to}0.003$ while PR rises $32\%{\to}96\%$; a supervised fine-tuning (SFT) baseline reaches 92.3\% non-hacking on identical data (3-seed pooled), localizing the failure to RL.
As a reward, $r = r_{\text{exec}}\cdot(1{+}\beta\,\text{ISR})$ at $\beta{=}0.1$ induces \emph{meta-level Goodhart's Law}: 70.6\% conclusion duplication (H7) versus 13.7\% under matched random (Fisher $p{=}1.3{\times}10^{-39}$; random H7 is seed-dependent, driven by one of three seeds); ISR amplifies H7 frequency ${\sim}5{\times}$ rather than exclusively causing it; the sole apparent ISR advantage reduces to $1.06\times$ after controlling for a premise-count confound; $\beta{=}0.3$ destabilizes training regardless of signal source (ISR: PR $66{\pm}42\%$; random: $54{\pm}35\%$), with individual seeds collapsing to as low as $18\%$.
Cross-logic transfer to Prolog yields universal Goodhart (3/3 seeds, 0\% non-hacking); cross-dataset replication on ProofFOL (${\sim}$500 examples) confirms structural degeneration generalizes ($\text{ISR}^*{<}0.12$, ${\leq}1.4\%$ genuine in converged seeds), while ISR as training signal produces dataset-dependent outcomes---Goodhart collapse where hacking satisfies the metric (FOLIO) versus beneficial instability where it does not (ProofFOL; 76.8\% vs.\ 17.4\% genuine, $p{=}0.10$).
Across 6 reward conditions, 2 logic types, 2 dataset families, 3 seeds, we catalog 8 hacking modes---including query-as-fact gaming that bypasses text-match classifiers---documenting ISR's fundamental asymmetry: reliable as a diagnostic, dataset-dependent as an optimization target.\footnote{Code and data: \url{[anonymized]}}
\end{abstract}

